:: Glowing Things, Sire {"position":"3500,1400","size":"100,100"}
SPEAKER: Gnarl
EMOTION (unlock): Composed
POPUP (unlock): Magic should cling to something sharp, Master! Restore the room.
START LOG: Steel is useful, Master, but glowing steel is better! Set an enchanting table here, Sire. Then perfectly good equipment can acquire stolen virtues without asking their former owners. Sharpness, fortune, flame... whatever behaves badly enough to bind! Magic should not float about looking mysterious, my Lord. Attach it to something sharp. Much more practical! The Empire would have called that dangerous, Sire. The Empire is dead. I think the argument settled itself rather nicely! Restore the room, and let our weapons become wonderfully unfair.
OBJECTIVES (voiced): Set an enchanting table here, Sire.
EMOTION (complete): Pleased
POPUP (complete): Excellent, Master! Perfectly good equipment may now become unfair.
COMPLETION LOG: Ah, listen to it hum, Sire! Symbols glow, and ordinary equipment becomes unfair. Excellent, Master! A sharp blade is a threat. A blade carrying impossible force becomes policy, my Lord. Do experiment. Half the pleasure lies in discovering what an enchantment does! The other half lies in not being its previous owner. Feed this room suitable power, Sire, with poor judgment in measured quantities. Soon even ordinary tools will acquire alarming personalities and deeply unfair advantages! That is progress, at least by every sensible Evil standard.
CODER NOTES: overlord:the_dark_tower/glowing_things_sire · working title "Enchantment Room" · trigger/objective: quest-specs part 1 · VOICE: O1 Tower_Forge 105.20

[[Home, Wretched Home]]

Enchantment Restoration


:: Evil Always Finds a Way {"position":"3400,2100","size":"100,100"}
SPEAKER: Gnarl
END SCREEN:
And so the Anchor fell. Ending, as such powers often do, in shattered crystal, scorched stone, and a dragon that would guard nothing again. The keeper of the wound was dead. The wound itself endured. The long Silence had ended at last, not with a prayer or a trumpet, but beneath the hand of the fifth Overlord. Across the old lands, bargains held, chains tightened, and ruins smoked according to his choosing. Forgotten powers stirred. Older scars split open. Yet the Tower stood, the Minions served, and a new reign had written itself over three quiet centuries. History could call that restoration if it wished. I knew better. It was conquest returning to its proper profession.
VARIANT [faction_spree=Peaceful]: Spree fattened happily on trade with the Tower. Its farmers kept their fields and its merchants kept smiling. Prosperity became the neatest chain of all, because everyone insisted the arrangement was simply good business.
VARIANT [faction_spree=Submission]: Spree kept its markets, beds, and watchtowers. Its guards merely learned whose authority stood behind their uniforms. The town called itself protected, while every familiar shield reminded it who permitted that safety.
VARIANT [faction_spree=Destruction]: Spree's guards fell, then its iron guardian. The town survived, but the bedtime story became civic memory again. Sheep grazed, beer poured, and comfortable streets remembered why Overlords were once whispered about.
VARIANT [faction_spree=unset]: Spree remained what three quiet centuries had made it: prosperous, provincial, and very pleased with itself. Sheep, pumpkins, beer, and loyal potatoes filled the days while the question of its place beneath the new reign remained unanswered.
VARIANT [escalated_spree=yes]: Spree trusted the arrangement. The Master answered that trust with ruin, and prosperity bought no mercy.
VARIANT [faction_bastille=Peaceful]: The Bastille kept its walls and its appetite for raiding. A boundary pact merely turned that appetite outward. Its warriors called the arrangement independence, while their approved victims learned how flexible that word could become.
VARIANT [faction_bastille=Submission]: The Bastille kept banners, battlements, and sour pride. Its horn no longer belonged entirely to it. When the Master's breath called, the beasts answered, and the raiders discovered command had changed hands without changing uniforms.
VARIANT [faction_bastille=Destruction]: The Bastille tested its oldest law: strength decides. Ten fighters and its clay guardian supplied the verdict. Enough stone remained for witnesses, leaving the fortress less a rival than a warning with battlements.
VARIANT [faction_bastille=unset]: The Bastille remained armed, loud, and magnificently certain that walls settled every argument. Its raiders still looked outward for weakness, while the new reign left their pride unmeasured and their allegiance inconveniently undecided.
VARIANT [escalated_bastille=yes]: So much for the horn's promise: the Bastille served, then learned obedience never guaranteed survival.
VARIANT [faction_successor_hold=Peaceful]: The Successor Hold kept its ancestral claim, carved neatly into stone. The Master gained the forge without wasting good metal on a siege. Dwarven continuity survived, now enjoying the inconvenient expense of a powerful patron.
VARIANT [faction_successor_hold=Submission]: The Hold preserved its halls, smiths, and proud old story. Its forge simply became a vassal forge. Dwarven armour still rang beneath the hammers, only now the line of continuity ended at a darker throne.
VARIANT [faction_successor_hold=Destruction]: The Hold's warriors died, and its Forger followed. The forge did not. Fire and craft survived their owners, fortunate indeed, because useful industry deserved preservation far more than Dwarven arguments about ownership.
VARIANT [faction_successor_hold=unset]: The Successor Hold continued disputing claims with ancestors who could no longer interrupt. Its forge burned, its pride endured, and nobody had yet settled whether ancient continuity outweighed the fifth Overlord standing inconveniently nearby.
VARIANT [escalated_successor_hold=yes]: What use was honoured vassalage when the forge still burned brighter after its masters died?
VARIANT [faction_kobolds=Peaceful]: The Den kept its tunnels and its talent for finding what history misplaced. Trade with its Captain bent the salvage route toward the Tower. Kobolds prospered, the Tower gained supplies, and distant Dwarven annoyance came free.
VARIANT [faction_kobolds=Submission]: Kobold engineers kept scavenging, tinkering, and making clever use of other peoples' ruins. Their best work now served the darker patron above them. They called it opportunity, which sounded much nicer than useful obedience.
VARIANT [faction_kobolds=Destruction]: The Captain died with ten Kobolds, and the Den lost its claim to the tunnels. Tools, passages, and useful scraps remained. Ownership did not. The salvagers had, with admirable symmetry, become salvage.
VARIANT [faction_kobolds=unset]: The Kobolds remained beneath the old workings, collecting whatever earlier peoples had neglected to secure. Their Den survived by clever hands and quick claims, while its relationship to the new reign stayed pleasantly unresolved.
VARIANT [escalated_kobolds=yes]: Epitaph for the Den: useful hands, clever tunnels, loyal service, and still not useful enough.
VARIANT [faction_gnumus=Peaceful]: Gnumu workshops hummed again under dark patronage. Old craft returned, trade grew, and full tables disguised dependence beautifully. They believed prosperity had restored their fortunes. The Tower merely noticed who had made that restoration possible.
VARIANT [faction_gnumus=Submission]: The Gnumus accepted credit, and arithmetic did what chains often do more noisily. Workshops expanded beneath a debt they could never politely forget. They kept eating, selling, and prospering while their future acquired a creditor with a throne.
VARIANT [faction_gnumus=Destruction]: The Gnumus finally found an appetite the reign would not feed. Their busy prosperity ended under violence instead of invoices. Workshops and stores outlived mouths that had mistaken abundance for security.
VARIANT [faction_gnumus=unset]: The Gnumus kept trading, eating, and polishing fragments of old craft into comfortable routine. Their forgotten origins stayed forgotten, their tables stayed busy, and no final bargain tied their prosperity to the fifth Overlord.
VARIANT [escalated_gnumus=yes]: A creditor remembers every debt. The Gnumus learned the throne could cancel accounts with fire.
VARIANT [faction_goblins=Peaceful]: The Goblins kept their caravans and their merchant pride. Deals remained full of cheerful fraud, which made peace almost respectable. Every profitable exchange drew the camp closer to the reign while both sides denied being cheated.
VARIANT [faction_goblins=Submission]: Goblin workshops smoked beneath a new patron. Their engineers still improvised loudly and congratulated themselves often, but the finest devices strengthened the Master's reign. Partnership was the preferred word. Obedience worked just as well.
VARIANT [faction_goblins=Destruction]: The Goblin camp learned family resemblance offered no protection. Traders, tinkerers, and noisy little cousins lost their claim beneath the Master's violence. The joke about kinship survived longer than their independence.
VARIANT [faction_goblins=unset]: The Goblins stayed greedy, inventive, and suspiciously familiar. Caravans moved, workshops rattled, and every bargain looked slightly dishonest. Their camp had not yet decided whether the new reign was customer, patron, enemy, or embarrassing relation.
VARIANT [escalated_goblins=yes]: Profits rose, workshops served, and kinship meant nothing once the Master chose ash over business.
VARIANT [faction_ribbits=Peaceful]: Ribbit gardens flourished beneath a tolerable arrangement. Crops moved outward, prosperity moved inward, and the frogs kept their swamp. I endured the music because useful food has excused worse noises than maracas.
VARIANT [faction_ribbits=Submission]: The Ribbits kept gardens, huts, and instruments. The rhythm changed ownership. Their maracas sounded when commanded, their labour served beyond the swamp, and the village learned that cheerful music can accompany very obedient hands.
VARIANT [faction_ribbits=Destruction]: The Ribbit village became wonderfully quiet. Gardens emptied, paths softened, and reeds reclaimed what the frogs had tended. Evernight's revived magic could grow another thicket. It could not argue for the dead.
VARIANT [faction_ribbits=unset]: The Ribbits continued gardening and making far too much music. Their swamp remained cheerful, productive, and offensively damp, while the fifth Overlord left their little village outside any settled bargain of peace, service, or ruin.
VARIANT [escalated_ribbits=yes]: The maracas had played on command. Their last lesson was quieter: cheerful obedience does not purchase tomorrow.
VARIANT [faction_mermorphs=Peaceful]: Aquamarine bought terms beneath the water. The Mermorphs kept their lanterns, ancestry, and dignified distance, while trade rose toward the surface. No drowned accusation needed reopening when glittering business already flowed in the proper direction.
VARIANT [faction_mermorphs=Submission]: The Mermorphs kept their village and their old elven memories. Their forges no longer worked only for themselves. Depth Metal rose from below for the Master's use, making submission unusually useful and pleasantly difficult to rust.
VARIANT [faction_mermorphs=Destruction]: The village beneath the waves lost the light its drowned ancestors had carried for centuries. The last glimmer of that old elven people went dark, leaving water, ruins, and no one to remember why.
VARIANT [faction_mermorphs=unset]: The Mermorphs remained below, carrying elven memories without remembering the disaster that drowned them. Their lights still moved through the water, their history stayed incomplete, and the fifth Overlord left their answer unwritten.
VARIANT [escalated_mermorphs=yes]: Depth Metal reached the Tower. Mercy never returned downstream, and the old elven light went out beneath it.
VARIANT [faction_piglins=Peaceful]: Piglin gold changed hands by bargain rather than theft. Disturbing, certainly, but efficient. Their villages kept bartering and boasting while the Master gained commerce without flattening relatives nobody was especially eager to acknowledge.
VARIANT [faction_piglins=Submission]: The Piglins kept mining, bartering, and admiring gold. Their treasury did not stay theirs. Once the Master emptied it, every shining ingot became a reminder that ownership belongs to whoever cannot be made to return it.
VARIANT [faction_piglins=Destruction]: Ten Piglins and two brutes settled the matter of family pride. Survivors kept whatever gold they could defend, along with an ancestry nobody wanted back. That family branch became considerably quieter.
VARIANT [faction_piglins=unset]: The Piglins remained proud, wealthy, and awkwardly related to an older Minion history. They guarded their gold, traded on their own terms, and enjoyed the rare luxury of being a family embarrassment not yet formally addressed.
VARIANT [escalated_piglins=yes]: Family discount, indeed. Gold changed hands first, then blood, and neither transaction earned a refund.
VARIANT [faction_grove=Peaceful]: Umvuthi kept his worshippers, feathers, and divine posture. A suitable offering bought his favour for the Master. The Grove called that sacred exchange. I preferred the simpler account: a god had rendered service and survived.
VARIANT [faction_grove=Submission]: The Grove kept its god above and its rites below. The mask changed the chain between them. Umvuthana followers still worshipped Umvuthi, but when command mattered, they answered the Master standing inconveniently higher.
VARIANT [faction_grove=Destruction]: Umvuthi fell, and sunlight failed to rebuild him. The Grove's masks, rites, and certainty survived only as leftovers. Divine permanence proved vulnerable to a determined Overlord. Not a phoenix after all.
VARIANT [faction_grove=unset]: The Grove remained full of masks, feathers, worship, and unanswered theology. Umvuthi still shone above his followers, while the fifth Overlord left unresolved whether a local god was neighbour, servant, prey, or merely future work.
VARIANT [escalated_grove=yes]: A god had blessed or bowed. Killing him afterward settled which altar had truly mattered.
VARIANT [faction_myrmex=Peaceful]: The Myrmex kept their Queen and their industrious mound. Resin purchased breathing room at an agreeable price. Workers hauled, soldiers guarded, and the colony continued believing survival was instinct rather than a negotiated expense.
VARIANT [faction_myrmex=Submission]: A command staff placed the colony's beautiful hierarchy beneath new direction. The Queen stayed, workers worked, soldiers marched, and nobody debated. For once, an entire society understood obedience without requiring speeches, prisons, or tedious philosophy.
VARIANT [faction_myrmex=Destruction]: The Queen died, and the colony's future died with her. Workers might still scurry and soldiers still bite, but the centre was gone. One royal death proved cleaner than stamping every tunnel.
VARIANT [faction_myrmex=unset]: The mound kept digging beneath the land, busy with pheromones, soldiers, workers, and one very important Queen. Its many legs remained outside the fifth Overlord's settled order, an unfinished claim scratching quietly underground.
VARIANT [escalated_myrmex=yes]: Resin, ranks, obedience. None saved the Queen when the Master decided the colony had outlived its bargain.
VARIANT [eidolon_path=Sacred]: The sacred rites reached the Mother Goddess, and she answered. Bright power crossed the old divide and entered the service of an Evil reign. I found the theology offensive and the result excellent. Even holiness, properly handled, can be made useful.
VARIANT [eidolon_path=Wicked]: The darker rites also found an answer. Whatever received them kept its name, face, and intentions wisely hidden. Power came anyway. I approved of the discretion, though not the mystery. Unseen patrons are tolerable only while they remember who benefits.
VARIANT [eidolon_path=unset]: No divine allegiance settled over the reign. Altars waited, offerings remained unclaimed, and the heavens received no preferred invitation. I considered this efficient. Gods become demanding once encouraged, and several centuries had already contained enough difficult landlords for any sensible dark household.
VARIANT [vampire=yes]: The Master's blood had changed. Immortality now carried its own appetite beneath the armour, elegant and inconvenient. Lestat noticed, of course, and took an interest. I trusted neither that interest nor the hunger behind it. Eternity is useful. It is never free.
VARIANT [vampire=no]: The Master's blood remained mortal, at least by the standards of creatures that enjoy counting such things. I preferred a hunger I already understood. Mortality had never stopped an Overlord before. It merely made good armour, loyal Minions, and timely resurrection more important.
VARIANT [side_lines_finished=0]: The side roads received nothing from him. Eighteen loose matters remained loose, from old scars to stranger tales. I called this focus only because calling it neglect in front of the Master seemed unnecessarily brave.
VARIANT [side_lines_finished=1]: One diversion earned the Master's full attention. Seventeen did not. A single finished thread was better than none, though I suspected even laziness should aspire to a more dramatic body count.
VARIANT [side_lines_finished=2]: Two side matters were settled and sixteen left muttering in the dark. The reign had clearly discovered curiosity, then misplaced it again. I recorded the effort with the generosity usually reserved for wounded Minions.
VARIANT [side_lines_finished=3]: Three lesser threads reached proper endings. Fifteen remained open, untidy, or dangerous. That was enough to prove the Master could be distracted, though not yet enough to make distraction look like policy.
VARIANT [side_lines_finished=4]: Four old scars and wandering tales were put in their place. Fourteen survived the attention. I began to suspect the Master enjoyed collecting unfinished business almost as much as everyone else collected trouble.
VARIANT [side_lines_finished=5]: Five side roads ended under the Master's boots. Thirteen still wandered elsewhere. A respectable start, certainly, though I had seen Minions empty a pantry with more complete strategic commitment.
VARIANT [side_lines_finished=6]: Six diversions were finished. Twelve remained to offend the Tower's sense of order. I grudgingly admitted this was becoming work rather than accident, which was praise of a very dangerous sort.
VARIANT [side_lines_finished=7]: Seven stray matters were dragged to conclusions. Eleven escaped. The Master was developing a taste for trouble beyond the main road, and I found that both wasteful and increasingly entertaining.
VARIANT [side_lines_finished=8]: Eight lesser threats, mysteries, and scars were settled. Ten remained abroad. Nearly half the side business had learned the correct lesson, and my complaints were becoming noticeably more ceremonial.
VARIANT [side_lines_finished=9]: Half the eighteen side threads were finished. The arithmetic was suspiciously neat. I approved despite myself, since nine resolved nuisances make a far better monument than nine unresolved ones.
VARIANT [side_lines_finished=10]: Ten detours reached their endings, leaving only eight unfinished. Curiosity had become policy somewhere along the road. I stopped calling the diversions pointless and began calling them useful, very quietly.
VARIANT [side_lines_finished=11]: Eleven side matters were closed and seven left standing. By then, few oddities could reasonably assume the Master's attention would pass them by. I considered that an excellent reputation to cultivate.
VARIANT [side_lines_finished=12]: Twelve of the eighteen side threads ended beneath the reign. Six remained. The Master had crossed from meddling into thoroughness, and I found myself running short of respectable objections.
VARIANT [side_lines_finished=13]: Thirteen roads away from the main road reached proper conclusions. Five did not. Unlucky numbers are mostly peasant superstition, but I was delighted by how unlucky thirteen had proved for everyone else.
VARIANT [side_lines_finished=14]: Fourteen wandering matters were settled, leaving four beyond the reckoning. Loose ends had become an endangered species. I approved of that sort of environmental damage with considerable enthusiasm.
VARIANT [side_lines_finished=15]: Fifteen side threads were finished. Only three remained outside the Master's grasp. By then, diversion had become dominion, and my earlier scolding looked increasingly like poor forecasting.
VARIANT [side_lines_finished=16]: Sixteen lesser roads reached their ends. Two escaped. I had to admit the Master's appetite for completion was becoming almost indecent, especially when so many conclusions involved corpses, relics, or ruined certainties.
VARIANT [side_lines_finished=17]: Seventeen of eighteen side matters were finished. One remained, which made the omission almost artistic. I disliked perfection on principle, but I disliked a single surviving loose end rather more.
VARIANT [side_lines_finished=18]: All eighteen side threads were finished. Every stray tale and Cataclysm scar received the Master's attention. I searched carefully for something to complain about and, for several alarming seconds, found nothing.
VARIANT [heart_seed=held]: The Master did not return empty-handed. A seed of the Tower Heart came home with him, a hard little promise carried out of the wound. Back at the Tower it called toward the greater Heart still lost beyond the rift. The answer was faint, but it was there. Home had found one fragment of itself, Sire, and that fragment knew the road continued outward.
VARIANT [heart_seed=not_held]: The Heart's fragment remained beyond the threshold. Its pulse stayed on the far side of the wound, unanswered by the Tower that had waited centuries for it. No seed came home to call toward the rest. That did not make the road vanish. It merely left one ancient summons hanging in the dark, Sire, waiting for the Master to decide that unfinished business had become intolerable.
Of course, no reign is permitted a tidy ending. Powers rise merely to test what ought to remain above them. Some crawl from black pits with ancient grudges. Others polish shining walls and mistake brightness for virtue. A few wait quietly, smiling, counting years, certain that patience makes them safe. Let them gather. Let them scheme. Thrones can empty. Towers can crack. Masters can vanish, return, and leave whole ages arguing over what happened. The fifth Overlord has already supplied the answer history keeps forgetting. Evil always finds a way.
CODER NOTES: overlord:the_wound/evil_always_finds_a_way · working title "End Credits" · REPLACES Minecraft's End Poem / credits screen at the same moment (player enters the End exit portal after the dragon dies, egg taken or not) · no popups, no objective, no portrait · assemble END SCREEN top to bottom: every fixed line, plus every VARIANT line whose tag matches the save's stat tags · show it as scrolling end-screen text, then save the identical full text as this quest's Questlog entry and complete the quest (this unlocks the postgame) · tags: faction_<11>=Peaceful|Submission|Destruction|unset; escalated_<faction>=yes (its line sits right after that faction's lines and only plays with Destruction); eidolon_path=Sacred|Wicked|unset; vampire=yes|no; side_lines_finished=0–18; heart_seed=held|not_held · VOICE: O2 End_Scene 100.10

[[It Didn't Close. Of Course.]]


:: Gluttony, Meet Grave {"position":"4900,1200","size":"100,100"}
SPEAKER: Gnarl
EMOTION (unlock): Darkened
POPUP (unlock): Enough cheese, Master. Let appetite meet something final.
START LOG: The Gnumus have eaten very well, Sire. Time they swallowed consequence! Kill ten of them, including one shaman. Do not lecture them about ancestry! They do not know it, and dead genealogies are dreadfully tedious. This is about the settlement before you, not the Halflings behind it. Their shamans dress appetite in magic. Their traders dress greed in business. Strip away both with the usual direct method! Ten deaths will break the confidence of the settlement. The shaman's death breaks the comforting idea that gluttony has sacred protection. Serve the final course, Master! No dessert!
OBJECTIVES (voiced): Kill ten Gnumus, including a Gnumu shaman, Sire.
EMOTION (complete): Relishing
POPUP (complete): Gluttony met the grave, Master. It appears the grave was hungrier.
COMPLETION LOG: Ten Gnumus are down, Sire, and their shaman joined them. The settlement's appetite has finally encountered something it cannot swallow. Good! Leave their forgotten ancestry buried. They died as Gnumus, with all the craft, greed, magic, and excessive eating that name had gathered. No ancient grudge was needed. Their present was quite irritating enough! The workshops may fall silent, the cheese may harden, and the doubloons can find more deserving pockets. Yours, for example. A short people built on appetite have reached a very short conclusion, Master. Ah, symmetry!
VARIANT [escalated_gnumus=yes]: They once prospered through your patronage or borrowed beneath your terms, Sire. Ending them now makes debt and gratitude equally poor shields against displeasing you.
CODER NOTES: overlord:the_reign/gluttony_meet_grave · working title "End the Gnumus" · trigger/objective: quest-specs part 2 (intros) + part 4 (paths) · TAG: faction_gnumus=Destruction · TAG when replacing Peaceful or Submission: escalated_gnumus=yes · VOICE: O1 EXP_D1_Abyss 301.10

Destruction Path


:: Clever Little Scavengers {"position":"5800,1200","size":"100,100"}
SPEAKER: Gnarl
EMOTION (unlock): Relishing
POPUP (unlock): Their engineers have tricks, Sire. Make those tricks report upward.
START LOG: Kobold engineers make very clever use of other people's leftovers, Master. Acquire one of their engineering templates and make the hierarchy clear. A pattern is more than scrap paper, Sire! It carries the Den's methods. It means their knowledge can travel where you require it! Do not destroy useful cleverness merely because it belongs to something knee-high. That would be wasteful! Take the template, Sire, and let the engineers understand who now benefits first from their work. The Den may keep its workshops! It simply gains a Master above the Captain. Very tidy, and much cheaper than digging new tunnels ourselves!
OBJECTIVES (voiced): Obtain a Kobold engineering template, Master.
EMOTION (complete): Pleased
POPUP (complete): Clever little scavengers, Sire! Now their cleverness serves uphill.
COMPLETION LOG: The template is yours, Master! Kobold engineering no longer stays buried in the Den. Their methods, tricks, and scavenged ingenuity can now serve your reign wherever needed. Let the Captain keep his chair, my Lord! Let the engineers keep their pride, Master. Neither changes the chain above them! Submission works best when useful institutions survive beneath it. These Kobolds understand salvage, adaptation, and making do. Excellent qualities in servants, Sire! They have spent generations turning abandoned Dwarf work into their own strength. Now you have turned Kobold work into yours. Ah, inheritance can be wonderfully contagious!
CODER NOTES: overlord:the_reign/clever_little_scavengers · working title "Engineer the Den" · trigger/objective: quest-specs part 2 (intros) + part 4 (paths) · TAG: faction_kobolds=Submission · VOICE: O1 D4_SD2_TempleConstruction 202.20

Submission Path


:: Bottled Sweetness. Ugh. {"position":"3100,3400","size":"100,100"}
SPEAKER: Gnarl
EMOTION (unlock): Relishing
POPUP (unlock): Essence of bees, Sire. Even the bottle looks smug.
START LOG: Take the hive's essence, Master. Yes, that golden thing! It reeks of light magic, sweetness, and cooperation. All deeply suspicious qualities. Unfortunately, it also reeks of power! Power remains useful even when insects approve of it. Bring the essence away from this hive, Sire. Let the bees keep flowers, honey, and community spirit! We take the concentrated part that fits in a bottle! Theft improves nearly every aesthetic disagreement. Besides, if they can make more, we have achieved something better than destruction. We have identified a renewable source of loot... revoltingly wholesome loot.
OBJECTIVES (voiced): Take the Essence of the Bees, Master, and bring it out.
EMOTION (complete): Pleased
POPUP (complete): Bottled sweetness... ugh, and valuable bottled sweetness, unfortunately!
COMPLETION LOG: The essence is yours, Sire! At last, compensation. We endured honey caverns, flowers, monarchy, and relentless buzzing. All for one useful concentration of magic, so call it a successful raid, Master! I shall never mention how pleasant anything smelled. The Empire failed to cleanse this pocket realm! You did something much more practical. Enter, learn its order, steal the valuable part, leave production intact! The bees can make more while we are elsewhere! Disgustingly sustainable Evil. I feel ill saying it. Put the bottle somewhere dark before the Tower begins looking cheerful.
CODER NOTES: overlord:tales/bottled_sweetness_ugh · working title "Essence of the Hive" · trigger/objective: quest-specs part 1 (hooks) + part 3 · STAT: side_lines_finished += 1 on completion · VOICE: O1 Tower_GeneralMessages 1404.20


:: Perfected Poison {"position":"2100,800","size":"100,100"}
SPEAKER: Blister
EMOTION (unlock): Relishing
POPUP (unlock): one good brew could be luck... I want luck unemployed.
START LOG: one good brew... maybe luck. second brew, maybe clever. I want no maybe, Sssire. pick one formula. same stuff, same heat, same result. no lucky bubble. no mystery smoke. if purple cloud comes... I know cause. if gums tingle... I know cause too. boring rules make useful poison. useful poison waits until right target. exciting poison bites brewer first. learned that. master one bottle until it keeps no secrets. then I almost trust it... almost.
OBJECTIVES (voiced): master one potion formula... same result, every time.
EMOTION (complete): Pleased
POPUP (complete): formula mastered... bottle finally stopped lying.
COMPLETION LOG: formula obeys now... from first drop to last. less waste. fewer surprises. better surprises when wanted. same potion means same mistake for enemy... efficient, yes. I can repeat it without sniffing every breath. I still sniff first breath. habit. maybe survival. Den works proper now: extract, plan, brew, master. clean chain... suspiciously clean. tongue says copper again, but only faint. I call that improvement. bottle has no secrets. I still keep cork tight.
CODER NOTES: overlord:the_dark_tower/perfected_poison · working title "Evil's Formula" · trigger/objective: quest-specs part 1 · VOICE: O2 NW_B RES.GREEN6


:: Snuff the Last Light {"position":"6500,1200","size":"100,100"}
SPEAKER: Gnarl
EMOTION (unlock): Darkened
POPUP (unlock): One last elven light, Master. You know how I feel about unfinished work.
START LOG: The Mermorphs still keep a spark of old elven light beneath the sea. Persistent, isn't it? Break one of their sea lanterns and kill ten Mermorphs, Sire. Let symbol and people fall together! This is not Everlight itself, Master! The sealed island remains beyond us! This is the light carried by those who drowned outside its gates and the descendants who followed. Snuff it here, Master! Ten deaths make the loss unmistakable. The broken lantern finishes the message. I have endured elven glow in forests, temples, ruins... seeing it drown properly would be almost therapeutic!
OBJECTIVES (voiced): Break a sea lantern and kill ten Mermorphs, Sire.
EMOTION (complete): Relishing
POPUP (complete): No more glow, Master! The sea has finally learned darkness.
COMPLETION LOG: The lantern is broken, ten Mermorphs are dead, and the last elven light in their village has gone dark. Ahhh, Sire! Centuries beneath the water could not extinguish it, but you managed admirably. Let the sea close over the dead and the broken glow alike. Everlight remains sealed elsewhere, beyond this victory, but these descendants carry no spark from it now. Their village has lost the symbol that survived their drowning. Cruel? Certainly! Complete? Much more important, Sire! Darkness suits the depths better anyway, Master! Even the fish have known that for ages, my Lord.
VARIANT [escalated_mermorphs=yes]: They once bartered with you or forged beneath your command, Master. Extinguishing them afterward proves neither trade nor service could purchase permanent light.
CODER NOTES: overlord:the_reign/snuff_the_last_light · working title "Extinguish the Light" · trigger/objective: quest-specs part 2 (intros) + part 4 (paths) · TAG: faction_mermorphs=Destruction · TAG when replacing Peaceful or Submission: escalated_mermorphs=yes · VOICE: O1 D4_SD5_TheRoyalHalls 200.40

Destruction Path


:: More to Conquer, Sire {"position":"4200,2100","size":"100,100"}
SPEAKER: Gnarl
EMOTION (unlock): Relishing
POPUP (unlock): Catacombs and a dark tower ahead, Sire! The wound shows ambition.
START LOG: Farther out, two structures deserve your attention, Master. Ancient catacombs cut into older scars, and a dark tower rises beyond them! Reach both. I want to know whether they belonged to early survivors or later inhabitants who learned to build around the wound. Either answer gives us a frontier, Sire, not an ending. Much healthier for Evil! Endings encourage retirement, gardening, and other wasting illnesses. Follow the roads beyond the city! Search the catacombs. Reach that tower! Far horizons are only borders that have not yet learned your name.
OBJECTIVES (voiced): Reach the far catacombs and dark tower beyond the wound's cities.
EMOTION (complete): Relishing
POPUP (complete): There, Sire! Catacombs, tower, and a frontier still refusing your name.
COMPLETION LOG:
You reached both structures, Master! The catacombs and dark tower sit among deeper scars. This wound has had centuries to become a country! Whoever built here endured pressures the Empire barely understood. That makes these ruins useful, Sire! They are not an epilogue! They are a frontier, once inhabited and gloriously unfinished. Search them when you wish! There are more histories here to steal. More distant things are waiting to learn they belong to you! Old pressure sits in every passage. Old builders left their stubbornness in the stone!
VARIANT [heart_seed=held]: The Heart's seed still calls farther outward, Master. Most of the Heart remains beyond.
VARIANT [heart_seed=not_held]: The Heart remains somewhere beyond, Sire, sensed once when the Anchor died.
CODER NOTES: overlord:the_wound/more_to_conquer_sire · working title "Far Edge of the Reign" · trigger/objective: quest-specs part 2 + narrative-beats · VARIANT: heart_seed=held|not_held · VOICE: O1 Tower_ThroneRoomMisc 200.40


:: Let Giblet Hit Things {"position":"3300,1400","size":"100,100"}
SPEAKER: Giblet the Sixth
EMOTION (unlock): Relishing
POPUP (unlock): Forge empty. Bad! Giblet needs anvil, Master.
START LOG: Forge empty. Giblet hates quiet iron. Proper anvil needed. Heavy. Honest. Loud. Giblet line hammered for old Masters. Giblet hammers for this one. First burnt. Third bled out. Fifth gibbed. Sixth still working. Good lineage! Put smithing anvil here, Master. Fire gets hot. Iron gets manners. Giblet builds blades, armour, chains, cages, sharp things, heavy things. Wall shakes? Wall weak. Giblet keeps hammering. Anvil gets first greeting. Then hammer starts proper conversation.
OBJECTIVES (voiced): Put smithing anvil here, Master. Giblet gets hammer!
EMOTION (complete): Pleased
POPUP (complete): Good anvil! Loud anvil! Giblet works proper.
COMPLETION LOG: Good anvil. Heavy anvil. Giblet approves! Forge has beat again. Iron learns shape here. Broken things learn new jobs. Giblet line kept hammer work alive. First burnt. Third bled out. Fifth gibbed. Sixth hits harder. Master brings iron. Master brings problems. Giblet treats both same. Hit until useful. Hit once more for certainty. Sparks burn roof? Roof too low. Not Giblet fault. Good forge makes noise. Quiet forge means something wrong.
CODER NOTES: overlord:the_dark_tower/let_giblet_hit_things · working title "Fire & Iron" · trigger/objective: quest-specs part 1 · VOICE: O2 NW_F FOUND.I_AMB1

